analysis:
This batch contains documentation changes only. In docs/provider/two-mac-cluster.md (head 536-583), the manual-update guidance for a cluster pair is replaced by a description of a coordinated pair auto-update. The update runs at a slot derived from the cluster ID, gated by auto_update and DARKBLOOM_NO_UPDATE_CHECK. Each Mac downloads, verifies and stages the release before restarting. The pair drains for 120s and never cancels accepted requests. A release one Mac cannot verify is installed on neither Mac. Rollback and quarantine follow the watchdog behavior (three self-ending starts within 10 minutes), and there is a pre-restart start check. darkbloom update coordinates with the peer and waits up to 15 minutes. Head 644-645 notes that local-API and solo serving do not drain for an update. In docs/reference/configuration.md (line 410), DARKBLOOM_NO_UPDATE_CHECK now also lists ClusterServiceCommand.swift supervisorConfiguration as a reader.

This behavior relates to T-043: rollback authority still sits in the same replaceable binary, now also in the cluster service. It relates to T-013/T-034/T-054 for release verification and qualification, since the pair re-registers under the new release's cluster approval, and to T-010 for drain without cancellation. Unchanged text at head 464-504 (prefix cache per tenant, encrypted SSD, T-041) is consistent with T-041's cacheSalt scoping.

No implementation source was supplied, so the docs' claims about these points are not verified:
- signature and hash verification of the staged release;
- the all-or-neither install;
- slot derivation;
- quarantine;
- whether the cluster service's updater honors the same signature, team-ID and downgrade checks as ProviderLoop+AutoUpdate/WatchdogCommand.

Integration should examine ClusterServiceCommand.swift (supervisorConfiguration, update and drain path) and the cross-cable peer messaging that triggers a restart. That review should check whether a peer can force a restart or downgrade, and whether a quarantined release can be re-offered. The threat model, T-043 in particular, may need updating to cover the cluster updater as a new update/rollback actor. No concrete regression is evidenced by the docs alone.
